window.docContents[174].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-edge-and-vertex-connectivity":{"contents":"Informally, a graph is k-edge-connected if deleting less than k edges always\nleaves it connected, and k-vertex-connected if deleting less than k vertices\nalways leaves it connected. Mathlib has the first notion, and we define the second.\n\nThese are the deletion forms of the definitions. Menger's theorem\nK. Menger, Zur allgemeinen Kurventheorie [On the general theory of curves], Fund. Math. 10 (1927), 96–115. gives the equivalent path forms: a graph is k-edge-connected\niff any two distinct vertices are joined by k edge-disjoint paths, and a graph with\nmore than k vertices is k-vertex-connected iff any two distinct vertices are\njoined by k paths that share only their endpoints. Menger's theorem is not\ncurrently in Mathlib.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.4. Edge and Vertex Connectivity","id":"/Connectivity/#section-edge-and-vertex-connectivity"}});